## Talking therapies

- **Psychoeducation** helps you understand cyclothymia and spot early signs of a mood change.
- **Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT)** helps you manage thoughts and behaviours linked to highs and lows.
- **Interpersonal and social rhythm therapy** focuses on keeping routines, including sleep, steady.

## Medicines

Not everyone needs medicine. If your moods have a big effect on your life, a psychiatrist may suggest a **mood stabiliser**, such as lithium or lamotrigine, or another medicine used in bipolar disorder.

Antidepressants on their own are used with care, because they can sometimes trigger a high.

## Ongoing support

Regular reviews with your GP or mental health team help catch any change early, including signs that cyclothymia may be developing into bipolar disorder.

Next, read about [living well with cyclothymia](/conditions/mental-health/cyclothymia/living-well/).
